PKG(1)                      User Commands                    PKG(1)

NAME
    pkg - install, remove, list and build packages

SYNOPSIS
    pkg [--prefix dir] [--root dir] [--config file] install file|name[-version]...
    pkg [--prefix dir] [--root dir] [--config file] check file|name[-version]...
    pkg [--prefix dir] [--root dir] [--config file] update
    pkg [--prefix dir] [--root dir] [--config file] search [pattern]
    pkg [--prefix dir] [--root dir] [--config file] upgrade [name...]
    pkg [--prefix dir] remove [--force] name...
    pkg [--prefix dir] list
    pkg [--prefix dir] info name|file
    pkg [--prefix dir] verify [name...]
    pkg [--root dir] build dir [file]

DESCRIPTION
    pkg installs applications from package files and from signed
    repositories into /home/.local and keeps a record of every installed
    file. A package is a gzip compressed ustar archive named
    name-version.mpk whose first member is the manifest and whose files
    lie under files/, relative to the prefix. Every check of an
    installation runs before anything is written: the dependencies, the
    conflicts, the shared libraries with their ABI numbers and symbols,
    and the ownership of every path.

    A repository is a directory served over plain HTTP that holds the
    archives, an index and the Ed25519 signature of the index. The index
    lists every archive with its name, version, dependencies, size and
    SHA-256 digest. Since HTTP offers no protection, pkg uses an index
    only when its signature verifies against a key in /etc/pkg/keys, and
    an archive only when its size and digest equal those in the index.

COMMANDS
    install   install or upgrade packages; an argument that is an
              existing file, contains a slash or ends in .mpk is a
              package file, any other names a package in the
              repositories, as name or name-version, and its
              dependencies are resolved through the indexes
    check     run the checks of install without writing anything; a
              package file is also compared with the entry of the same
              name and version in the indexes
    update    fetch the index of every repository, verify its signature
              and keep it under /home/.local/lib/pkg/_repos; a refused
              index leaves the previous one in place
    search    print the entries of the indexes whose name or summary
              contains pattern, one name, version, repository and
              summary per line
    upgrade   install the higher versions that the indexes offer for
              the installed packages, or for the named ones
    remove    remove installed packages; --force removes a package that
              other packages depend on
    list      print the installed packages, one name, version and
              summary per line
    info      print the manifest and the files of an installed package
              or of a package file
    verify    compare the recorded size and checksum of every installed
              file with the file on disk
    build     write a package from a directory holding manifest and
              files/; the needs lines are derived from the ELF files

OPTIONS
    --prefix dir   install under dir instead of /home/.local
    --root dir     read lib/abi, the system libraries, etc/pkg.conf and
                   etc/pkg/keys below dir
    --config file  read the repositories from file instead of
                   /etc/pkg.conf

CONFIGURATION
    /etc/pkg.conf names the repositories, one per line, in the order in
    which they are searched, and may set the timeout in seconds for the
    connection and for every wait for data (30 by default):

        repo build http://10.0.2.2:8000
        timeout 30

    A key file in /etc/pkg/keys has the suffix .pub and holds one line,
    ed25519 followed by the public key in hexadecimal. The image carries
    the key of the build as /etc/pkg/keys/build.pub.

EXIT STATUS
    pkg exits with 0 on success and 1 when an operation failed. A
    refused signature, an archive that differs from the index, a
    connection that fails or times out, an HTTP status other than 200
    and a response shorter or longer than its Content-Length are
    failures, and each prints a message naming the repository or the
    package. An unknown command or missing arguments exit with 2.

BUNDLED APPLICATIONS
    Optional applications are available as archives in /usr/share/packages.
    Install selected archives, or all of them, with the examples below.
    They are not installed automatically. The package prefix persists on
    the data volume across root image rebuilds. make repo writes the same
    archives as a signed repository to build/repo on the host.

FILES
    /etc/pkg.conf                        the repositories
    /etc/pkg/keys/*.pub                  the trusted public keys
    /home/.local/lib/pkg/name/manifest   the manifest as installed
    /home/.local/lib/pkg/name/files      path, size and CRC-32 of each file
    /home/.local/lib/pkg/name/dirs       the directories the package created
    /home/.local/lib/pkg/_repos/repo/    the verified index of a repository
    /home/.local/share/launcher          launcher entries of the packages
    /home/.local/share/mime.types        MIME types of the packages
    /home/.local/share/mime.apps         MIME handlers of the packages
    /lib/abi                             ABI numbers of the system libraries

EXAMPLES
    pkg install /usr/share/packages/calc-*.mpk
    pkg install /usr/share/packages/*.mpk
    pkg build pong
    pkg install pong-1.2.0.mpk
    pkg update
    pkg search calc
    pkg install calc
    pkg install calc-0.2.0
    pkg upgrade
    pkg check calc-0.2.0.mpk
    pkg list
    pkg remove pong

SEE ALSO
    http(1), tar(1), gzip(1), ld(1)
